<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Vesuvius</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vesuvius, Vesaevus, Vesevus, Vesbius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Vesuvius is a somma-stratovolcano on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Gulf of Naples</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, about 9 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Its eruption of 79 CE destroyed the Roman cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herculaneum">Herculaneum</a>, Oplontis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stabiae">Stabiae</a>, throwing a cloud of stones, ash and gas to a height of 33 km; more than 1,000 people are thought to have died, and the only surviving witness account is two letters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> held the mountain sacred to Hercules. It has erupted many times since, most recently from 1913 to 1944, and is counted among the most dangerous volcanoes in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius.jpg" alt="Mount Vesuvius" width="330" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: pietro scerrato · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Il_cratere_del_Vulcano_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vesuvius is a humpbacked peak: a large cone, the Gran Cono, partly encircled by the steep rim of a summit caldera left by the collapse of an earlier and much higher structure, Mount Somma. The caldera began to form in an eruption around 17,000 to 18,000 years ago, and the Gran Cono was produced in the eruption of 79 CE, which is why the volcano is also called Somma-Vesuvius. The summit stands 1,281 m above sea level. The volcano formed where the African plate is subducted beneath the Eurasian plate, and it is one of several volcanoes in the Campanian volcanic arc, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campi-flegrei">Campi Flegrei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the time of the 79 CE eruption it was regarded as a divinity of the Genius type and appears as a serpent in frescoes of household shrines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a>; an inscription from Capua names Jupiter Vesuvius. The eruption was preceded by a powerful earthquake in 62, which damaged Pompeii. Small tremors began four days before the eruption but the warnings were not recognised. The eruption destroyed Pompeii, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herculaneum">Herculaneum</a>, Oplontis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stabiae">Stabiae</a>; the first two were buried under tens of metres of tephra and struck by pyroclastic surges. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a> described it, including the death of his uncle.</p><p>Later eruptions came in 203, 472, when ash fell on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and 512, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> exempted those living on the slopes from taxes. In December 1631 a major eruption buried many villages under lava flows and killed around 3,000 people. The eruption of 5 April 1906 killed more than 100 people, and the funds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> had set aside for the 1908 Olympic Games went to rebuilding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, so the Games moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The volcano was active from 1913 through 1944, and the eruption of March 1944 destroyed the villages of San Sebastiano al Vesuvio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> di Somma and Ottaviano. It has not erupted since.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The area around Vesuvius was declared a national park on 5 June 1995. The summit is open to visitors, with road access to within 200 metres of the summit in height and a spiral walkway from the road to the crater. The volcano is still regarded as active, though its present activity produces little more than sulfur-rich steam from vents in the crater. About 3,000,000 people live near enough to be affected by an eruption, and the volcano is one of the Decade Volcanoes. Its first funicular opened in 1880 and was destroyed in 1944; the song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà</a> commemorated its opening.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Vesuvius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It celebrated the opening that year of the first funicular railway on <b>Mount Vesuvius</b>, and it was Turco who prompted Denza to write it, perhaps as a joke.</blockquote><a href="/songs/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà</a></li><li><blockquote>By the traditional date, <b>Mount Vesuvius</b> erupted and buried Pompeii and Herculaneum under ash and pumice, killing thousands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-24">24 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
